    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "A key concept that helps us to solve Problem 1 efficiently is the computational geometry notion of k-th level of arrangement [21]. Consider the dual hyperplanes H={d(t1),\u00b7\u00b7\u00b7,d(tn)}. The dissection of the dual space with the hyperplanes is called the arrangement",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 2": {
    "total_score": 0.0011210713713670033,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "0.000.250.500.751.001.251.501.752.000.000.250.500.751.001.251.501.752.00 t1 t2t3rf(a) Primal space: [t2,t1,t3]shown as points, along with their projection on the ray rf. 0.000.250.500.751.001.251.501.752.000.000.250.500.751.001.251.501.752.00 d(t1)d(t2)d(t3)rf(b) Dual space: The intersection of dual hyperplanes of the points with a ray rf. 0.00 0.25 0.50 0.75 1.00 1.25 1.50 1.75 2nd Level of Arrangement(c) 2nd level of arrangements in the dual space high- lighted as the orange line segments. the arrangement in the first quadrant. The order of projection in Fig. 2a is the reverse of the intersection of dual hyperplanes with rf ([d(t3),d(t1),d(t2)]) in Fig. 2b.",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 3": {
    "total_score": 0.025504373698599325,
    "has_citations": true,
    "text": "In Fig. 2c, the dotted blue lines indicate the boundaries of the median regions (the change in the line segment indicates a change in the median (2nd point) of Df). AofH, which we simply call the arrangement. The intersection of \u03b4\u2208[d]hyperplanes inHforms a (d\u2212\u03b4)-face of the arrangement. In particular, the intersection of dhyperplanes is a (0)-face (a point), referred to as a vertex of the arrangement, while the (1)-faces (line segments) connecting two vertices are called the edges . We call two vertices that share an edge as neighbors . Please refer to [ 21] for more details about the arrangement. Now consider the origin-anchored ray rf\u20d7in the dual space and the intersections of the dual hyperplanes with it. Based on Lemma 1, thek-th closest intersection to the origin specifies the tuple with thek-th largest value in Df. We define the universe of all such intersection points for all vectors fas thek-th level of arrangement, referred asAk. InR2, we showAkas the ordered list of the vertices (the intersection points) of the k-th level, in counter-clock-wise (fromx-axis toy) or clock-wise.",
    "citation_focus_text": "AofH, which we simply call the arrangement. In particular, the intersection of dhyperplanes is a (0)-face (a point), referred to as a vertex of the arrangement, while the (1)-faces (line segments) connecting two vertices are called the edges . Please refer to [ 21] for more details about the arrangement."
  },
  "Paragraph 4": {
    "total_score": 0.0024661866414190546,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "For example, Fig. 2c shows (in orange) the 2nd level of an ar- rangement of 3 (toy) tuples in the dual space in R2. In this example, A2=\u27e80,\u00d71,2,\u00d71,3,\u03c0 2\u27e9, where\u00d7i,jis the intersection between d(ti) andd(tj). One can see that the k-th level is a chain of line segments forming a non-convex shape. In addition, the set of top- ktuples in each line segment is fixed and is different from the neighboring line segments. Moreover, whenk=n 2, thek-th level of the arrangement tracks the median for any possible direction f. Specifically, the intersection ofAn 2with a rayrf\u20d7is a line segment, specifying the median ofDf. Evidently, the median of all rays intersecting with the same line segment inAn 2is the same. Following this observation, we define the notion of median regions as follows. Definition 1 (Median Region). A median region is the range of raysrf\u20d7, for which the median of the projection Dfis the same. The partitioning of the origin-anchored rays in the dual space according to the(d\u22121)-dimensional simplexes (line-segments in 2D) ofAn 2provides the set of all median regions. Specifically, in 2D each median region is a range that begins/ends at the beginning/end of each line segment (e.g., see Fig. 2c). Moreover, since within every median region, no dual hyperplane intersects with the median simplex, the set of tuples with projection values being larger (resp. smaller) than the median does not change.",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 5": {
    "total_score": 0.01877794547039731,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "For example, in Fig. 2c,consider the median region between the x-axis and the first (bottom) blue ray. d(t2)is the median line-segment. The intersection of d(t3) with any ray in this region remains below the median (closer to the origin). Similarly, the intersection of d(t1)above the median. Using these observations, one can write a linear program for every median region to find the maximum skew within a median region. Instead, we introduce Theorem 2 as a key observation that enables a more efficient algorithm in Section 4. Let \u03bc(D) be the vector in Rdthat contains the mean of D, i.e.,\u03bc(D)=1 n\u2211\ufe01n i=1ti. We defineqi=ti\u2212\u03bc(D). In other words, qi\u2019s are the mean-normalized tuples ofD. LetQbe ad\u00d7nmatrix where each column of Qisq\u22a4 i. For a unit vector f, lettmfbe the median ofDafter projection on f. In other words, t\u22a4mffis the median ofDf. Theorem 2 helps us to find the high skew direction vector f\u2217efficiently4: Theorem 2. For a given median region with median tmf, the max skew direction f\u2217either falls in the boundaries of this median region or it is equal to(QQ\u22a4)\u22121qmf |(QQ\u22a4)\u22121qmf|.",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  }
}
